course: biology
lesson: 359
title: Bones by name: hips, legs and feet, and the extra bones of a baby
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Your legs carry your whole weight all day, so their bones are the biggest you have: the femur in the thigh is the longest and strongest bone in the body, about a quarter of your height.
explain: Each hip bone looks like one bone but is really three fused together, and together the hips form the pelvis, a bowl that passes your weight down to your legs and shelters organs inside.
explain: Step out of the bath onto a dry floor and look at your wet footprint: the missing bit on the inside edge is your arch, a spring made of many small foot bones.
explain: A newborn baby has about 270 to 300 bones, more than you; as it grows, many of them join together, until a grown-up has 206.
fact: The femur, or thigh bone, is the longest and strongest bone in the body, about a quarter of a person's height.
fact: The kneecap protects the front of the knee and helps the thigh muscles pull; in babies it is soft cartilage that turns to bone over the first years.
fact: The shin bone, the tibia, lies just under the skin with little padding, which is why kicks hurt and footballers wear shin guards; the thin fibula runs beside it.
fact: Each hip bone is three bones fused together; the two hip bones and the base of the spine form the pelvis, which carries the upper body's weight to the legs and protects organs.
fact: Each foot has 26 bones, so both feet have 52; hands and feet together hold over half of all the body's bones.
fact: The arch of the foot works like a spring; toddlers have flat feet and grow arches later, and some people keep flat feet with no problems.
fact: The heel bone is the largest bone of the foot; it takes the shock of each step, and the Achilles tendon attaches to it.
fact: In many people two of the little toe's bones are fused, so it has only two bones instead of three; this is common and harmless.
fact: A newborn has about 270 to 300 bones, many of which fuse as it grows, leaving 206 in an adult; the skull and the base of the spine have many fused parts.
fact: Growth plates near the ends of bones are where the bones grow longer; they show as dark lines on an X-ray and close in the late teens, after which you stop getting taller.
fact: A broken growth plate needs a doctor's care, because if it heals badly that bone may grow less.
exercise: Which is the longest bone in the body? | The femur in the thigh | The shin bone in the lower leg | The humerus in the upper arm | The long spine in the back
exercise: About how long is the femur compared with your height? | About a quarter of your height | About half your height | About a tenth of your height | Exactly as long as your arm
exercise: What does the kneecap do? | Protects the knee and helps the thigh muscles pull | Joins the top of the thigh bone onto the side of the hip | Holds the foot bones together | Makes the leg bend sideways
exercise: What is a baby's kneecap made of at first? | Soft cartilage | Hard bone like a grown-up's | A little pad of fat | Nothing, babies have no knees
exercise: Why does a kick on the shin hurt so much? | The shin bone lies just under the skin with little padding | The shin is the weakest and thinnest bone in the whole body | The shin has no nerves | The shin is hollow inside
exercise: How many bones fuse to make one hip bone? | Three | One | Two | Six
exercise: What does the pelvis do? | Carries the upper body's weight to the legs and protects organs | Pumps the blood all the way down to the legs and the feet and back up again | Holds the ribs in place | Makes the feet grow
exercise: How many bones are in one foot? | 26 | 27 | 5 | 52
exercise: What does the arch of the foot do? | Works like a spring | Stores fat for walking | Keeps the toes warm in winter | Holds the toenails on
exercise: What kind of feet do toddlers have? | Flat feet that grow arches later | High arches that slowly flatten out later | Feet with no bones yet | Feet with all bones fused
exercise: Which is the largest bone of the foot? | The heel bone | The big toe bone | The little toe bone | The round kneecap
exercise: What is often unusual about the little toe? | Two of its bones are often fused | It has no bones at all | It has five bones | It is made of cartilage for life
exercise: About how many bones does a newborn baby have? | About 270 to 300 | 206, the same as adults | About 100 | About 1,000
exercise: Why does a grown-up have fewer bones than a baby? | Many baby bones fuse together | Bones fall out as you grow | Bones melt into muscle | Grown-ups lose bones in their teeth
exercise: Where do bones grow longer? | At growth plates near their ends | Right in the middle of each long bone | Only in the skull | From the marrow outwards
exercise: When do growth plates close? | In the late teens | As soon as a baby is born | At about five years old | Only in very old age
test: Count them: both feet together have how many bones? | 52 | 26 | 54 | 106
test: Count them: both hands have 54 bones and both feet 52. How many bones is that altogether? | 106 | 96 | 108 | 206
test: If someone's femur is 40 centimetres long, about how tall are they? | About 160 centimetres | About 80 centimetres | About 40 centimetres | About 3 metres
test: «baby's got more bones than me? no way, she's tiny» What is right? | Yes, about 270 to 300, and many join as she grows | No, babies have no bones at all until they start to walk | No, babies have exactly 206 | Yes, and she keeps them all for life
test: In a cartoon Tom gets struck by lightning and his skeleton flashes up with just a few stick bones in each leg. How many bones are really in one foot? | 26 | 1 | 5 | 10
test: «my toddler cousin's got flat feet, he's gonna need special shoes forever» What is fairest to say? | Toddlers usually have flat feet and grow arches later | Flat feet in toddlers are always a disease that needs a doctor straight away | His feet will never grow arches | He needs surgery at once
test: An X-ray of a 12-year-old shows dark lines near the ends of the leg bones. What are they? | Growth plates where the bones are still growing longer | Cracks from a fall | Gaps where bones are missing | Shadows from the X-ray machine
test: «I'm 20, if I eat loads I'll keep growing taller» What is the truth? | Once growth plates close in the late teens, bones stop growing longer | Eating more always makes you taller, at any age, as long as you eat the right food | You grow taller until you are 50 | Only sleep makes adults taller
test: Why does the heel bone need to be the biggest bone of the foot? | It takes the shock of every step and anchors the Achilles tendon | It holds all the toes together | It has to store fat for walking | It is the only foot bone with marrow
test: A boy counts the bones in his little toe on an X-ray and finds two, not three. What should he think? | Nothing is wrong; two little-toe bones often fuse | His little toe must be broken and needs a plaster | He has lost a bone | The X-ray must be fake
test: Why might a baby's kneecap be soft cartilage at first? | Soft cartilage bends safely while the baby crawls and grows, and turns to bone later | Babies do not need knees | The kneecap is missing in babies | Babies' bones are all made of fat
exam: Identify the longest and strongest bone of the human skeleton. | The femur | The tibia of the shin | The humerus | The spinal column
exam: State the number of bones comprised in a single human foot. | Twenty-six | Twenty-seven | Fifty-two | Five
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diminution in the number of bones between infancy and adulthood? | Numerous bones present at birth fuse together in the course of growth | Bones are shed from the body in the course of growth | Bones are absorbed into the muscles | Bones are lost in consequence of the loss of the deciduous teeth
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning growth plates. | They permit the lengthening of bones and close in the late teens | They remain open throughout life | They are situated solely in the skull | They close at the moment of birth
